  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-112540",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $-9$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9}{x+3}={x}^{2}+x+3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)({x}^{2}+x+3).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+x+3$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={1}^{2}-4(1)(3)=-11<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+x+3$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)({x}^{2}+x+3).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
